-- emit: the derived `compare` of a NOMINAL type, every shape §9.4 lists
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §9.4, §8.5, A.12, A.20, A.61).
--
-- Nothing below uses `<` or `==` on any of these types. They are all
-- emitted anyway, because derivation for a nominal type is EAGER and
-- happens in the declaring module (§8.5): it is the only module that may
-- read a `pub opaque type`'s constructors, and the only one whose output
-- does not then depend on which other module asked first.
--
-- Six shapes, and one claim each:
--
--   - `Colour` — all nullary, so a value IS its tag string and `<` on the
--     tags would be ALPHABETIC. The `$order` table is what makes
--     declaration order the answer, and it is emitted in a pass of its own
--     AHEAD of every function (§8.5): it is an object literal, not an
--     arrow, so it has to precede its reader. `Colour$$eq` is still
--     written, and is `===`: a USE of `==` on a `Colour` gets
--     `primitive strict_eq` and never calls it (A.18), but a `Colour`
--     nested inside another shape names it as that position's evidence,
--     and an operator is not a value (§9.1).
--   - `Shape` — a payload, so every constructor is padded to `{$, a, b}`
--     and `Circle`'s `b` is `null`. Padding slots are NOT compared (A.12):
--     `Circle`'s arm reads `a` alone. The tag test comes first and orders
--     two different constructors through `$order`.
--   - `Wrapper` — one constructor, so no `$order` table and no tag test:
--     there is nothing to disagree about.
--   - `Tree` — recursive, and its own derived function is a position of
--     itself. A top-level `const` arrow may name itself from inside its
--     body, which is why the emission order of §8.5 never has to be a
--     dependency order.
--   - `Outcome x a` — one evidence parameter per type parameter in
--     DECLARATION order, used or not (A.20). `x` is quantified first, so
--     `$m$0` is `x`'s and `$m$1` is `a`'s — and `Ok a`, declared FIRST and
--     therefore `order.Ok = 0`, uses `$m$1`. Getting that pairing wrong is
--     the bug the `dispatch/` `part` lines exist to catch.
--   - `Label` — the two positions JavaScript's `<` gets wrong, INLINE.
--     Inside a body a `primitive` part is the operator itself (§9's parts
--     contract), so `Char` is a code-point comparison hoisted into two
--     `const`s and `String` is a call of `String$compare` — never `<`
--     (§3.2, A.26).
--
-- The `$$order` and `$$compare` bases take a double separator, which is
-- the one name no module path can spell (A.61).
import Node exposing (Program)


pub type Colour
    = Red
    | Green
    | Blue


pub type Shape
    = Circle Float
    | Rect Float Float


pub type Wrapper
    = Wrapper Int


pub type Tree
    = Leaf
    | Node Tree Tree


pub type Outcome x a
    = Ok a
    | Err x


pub type Label
    = Initial Char
    | Named String


main : Program
main =
    Node.printLines []
